\label{IqCge2}
Let $C$ be a Hermitian-relative curve. Suppose that $C$ has at least two rational inflexions. Then $C$ is projectively equivalent to the curve defined by
\begin{equation}\label{at_least_two_inflexions}
x^{\sqrt{q}}y + \eta xy^{\sqrt{q}} + z^{\sqrt{q}+1} = 0
\end{equation}
for some $\eta \in \mathbb{F}_q^{\ast}$. Conversely, any curve defined by \eqref{at_least_two_inflexions} has at least two rational inflexions. These curves are classified into the following three distinct types:
\begin{enumerate}[{\rm (i)}]
\item If $\eta = 1$, then the curve is Hermitian.
\item If $\eta^{\sqrt{q}+1} = 1$ with $\eta \neq 1$, then the curve \eqref{at_least_two_inflexions} has exactly $\sqrt{q}+1$ rational points, all of which are inflexions. In this case, the curve is projectively equivalent to
\begin{equation}\label{diagonaloftype1}
x^{\sqrt{q}+1} + y^{\sqrt{q}+1} + \eta' z^{\sqrt{q}+1} = 0
\end{equation}
for some $\eta' \in \mathbb{F}_q^{\ast} \setminus \mathbb{F}_{\sqrt{q}}^{\ast}$.
\item If $\eta^{\sqrt{q}+1} \neq 1$, then the curve \eqref{at_least_two_inflexions} has exactly $q+1$ rational points, of which exactly two are inflexions.
\end{enumerate}
Furthermore, this classification results in three distinct types of curves, as summarized in Table~\ref{Iqcge2}.

\label{Hilbert}
The kernels of the trace and norm maps are given as follows:
\begin{enumerate}[{\rm (i)}]
\item $\rm{Ker}(\mathrm{Tr}) = \{ \alpha - \alpha^{\sqrt{q}} \mid \alpha \in \mathbb{F}_q \}$.
\item $\rm{Ker}(\mathrm{Nm}) = \{ \alpha^{1-\sqrt{q}} \mid \alpha \in \mathbb{F}_q^{\ast} \}$.
\end{enumerate}

\label{1pt}
For a Hermitian-relative curve $C$, the following statements are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}[{\rm (a)}]
\item $N_q(C) = 1$.
\item $C$ is of type $(1,1)$.
\item $C$ is projectively equivalent to the curve $C_{A_\eta}$ defined by the equation
\begin{equation}\label{eq1pt}
x^{\sqrt{q}+1} + \eta y^{\sqrt{q}+1} + y^{\sqrt{q}} z + yz^{\sqrt{q}} = 0
\end{equation}
for some $\eta \in \mathbb{F}_q \setminus \mathbb{F}_{\sqrt{q}}$.
\end{enumerate}
Furthermore, any two curves satisfying these equivalent conditions are projectively equivalent.

By Proposition \ref{mirror}, if an $\mathbb{F}_q$-rational point $P$ is not an inflexion, then its tangent line must contain another $\mathbb{F}_q$-rational point $P' \in C(\mathbb{F}_q) \setminus \{P\}$. Thus, if $N_q(C) = 1$, the unique $\mathbb{F}_q$-point must be an inflexion, which shows that (a) implies (b). Conversely, (b) implies (a) by definition.

Now assume (c). 
Let $C$ be the curve defined by \eqref{eq1pt}. It is obvious that $P_0 = (0,0,1) \in C(\mathbb{F}_q)$. The tangent line to $C$ at $P_0$ is given by $y=0$, which contacts the curve with multiplicity $\sqrt{q}+1$. Thus, $P_0$ is an $\mathbb{F}_q$-rational inflexion of $C$. Now, let $(x_0 , y_0 , z_0) \in C(\mathbb{F}_q)$. Then we have
\begin{equation}\label{eq:trace_norm}x_0^{\sqrt{q}+1} + \eta y_0^{\sqrt{q}+1} + y_0^{\sqrt{q}} z_0 + y_0 z_0^{\sqrt{q}} = 0.
\end{equation}
Note that $x_0^{\sqrt{q}+1} = \mathrm{Nm}(x_0) \in \mathbb{F}_{\sqrt{q}}$ and $y_0^{\sqrt{q}} z_0 + y_0 z_0^{\sqrt{q}} = \mathrm{Tr}(y_0 z_0^{\sqrt{q}}) \in \mathbb{F}_{\sqrt{q}}$. Since $\eta \notin \mathbb{F}_{\sqrt{q}}$, the identity \eqref{eq:trace_norm} forces $y_0^{\sqrt{q}+1} = 0$, which implies $y_0 = 0$. Consequently, $x_0^{\sqrt{q}+1} = 0$, leading to $x_0 = 0$. Therefore, $P_0 = (0,0,1)$ is the unique $\mathbb{F}_q$-rational point of $C$, confirming that (a) holds.

Now we prove that (b) implies (c). 
Let $C$ be a Hermitian-relative curve of type $(1,1)$. We may assume that $P=(0:0:1)$ is the unique $\mathbb{F}_q$-rational point of $C$, which is also an inflexion, and that the line $y=0$ is the tangent line at $P$. Let $A=(a_{ij}) \in PGL(3,\mathbb{F}_q)$ be the matrix corresponding to $C$, i.e., $C=C_A$. Since $(0:0:1) \in C_A$, it follows that $a_{33}=0$. Because the line $y=0$ intersects the curve $C_A$ at $P$ with multiplicity $\sqrt{q}+1$, the polynomial
$$\begin{pmatrix} x^{\sqrt{q}} & 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} A \begin{pmatrix} x \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} = a_{11}x^{\sqrt{q}+1} + a_{13}x^{\sqrt{q}} + a_{31}x$$
must be a nonzero constant multiple of $x^{\sqrt{q}+1}$. This implies that $a_{11} \ne 0$ and $a_{13}=a_{31}=0$. Since $A\in PGL(3,\mathbb{F}_q)$, we may assume that 
$A=\begin{pmatrix}1&a_{12}&0\\ a_{21}&a_{22}&a_{23}\\ 0&a_{32}&0\end{pmatrix}$. Note that $a_{23} \ne 0$ and $a_{32} \ne 0$ since $\det A \ne 0$.

Let $T_1 = \begin{pmatrix} 1&-a_{12}&0\\ 0&1&0\\0&0&1\end{pmatrix} \in GL(3,\mathbb{F}_q)$ and let$$A_1 = T_1^* A T_1 = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\ a_{21}-a_{12}^{\sqrt{q}} & -a_{12}a_{21}+a_{22} & a_{23} \\ 0 & a_{32} & 0 \end{pmatrix}.$$
                                        
Next, let $T_2 = \begin{pmatrix} 1&0&0\\ 0&1&0\\0&0&a_{32}^{-\sqrt{q}}\end{pmatrix} \in GL(3,\mathbb{F}_q)$ and define
$$A_2 = T_2^* A_1 T_2 = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\ b_{21} & b_{22} & b_{23} \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix}$$
with $b_{21} = a_{21}-a_{12}^{\sqrt{q}}$, $b_{22} = a_{22}-a_{12}a_{21}$, and $b_{23} = a_{23}a_{32}^{-\sqrt{q}}$. Since $A$ and $A_2$ are congruent, the curve $C_A$ is projectively equivalent to $C_{A_2}$ by Lemma~\ref{cong}, where the latter is given by the equation
\begin{equation}
x^{\sqrt{q}+1} + b_{21}xy^{\sqrt{q}} + b_{22}y^{\sqrt{q}+1} + b_{23}y^{\sqrt{q}}z + yz^{\sqrt{q}} = 0.
\end{equation}
 
By the definitions of $T_1$ and $T_2$, $P=(0,0,1)$ is still the unique $\mathbb{F}_q$-rational point of $C_{A_2}$ with the tangent line $y=0$.

Since $P=(0,0,1)$ is the unique $\mathbb{F}_q$-rational point of $C_{A_2}$, the affine equation
\begin{equation}\label{y=1}
x^{\sqrt{q}+1} + b_{21}x + b_{22} + b_{23}z + z^{\sqrt{q}} = 0
\end{equation}
has no solutions in $\mathbb{F}_q^2$. For  $x_0 \in \mathbb{F}_q$, consider the following equation in $z$:
\begin{equation}\label{zdegsq}
z^{\sqrt{q}} + b_{23}z + (x_0^{\sqrt{q}+1} + b_{21}x_0 + b_{22}) = 0.
\end{equation}
Note that $b_{23} \ne 0$ because $\det A_2 \ne 0$. Since \eqref{zdegsq} has no solutions in $\mathbb{F}_q$, Proposition \ref{degsqrtq} implies that $\mathrm{Nm}(b_{23}) = 1$. Furthermore, as $\mathrm{Nm}(-b_{23}) =  \mathrm{Nm}(b_{23}) = 1$, it follows from Lemma \ref{Hilbert} that there exists an element $\rho \in \mathbb{F}_q^*$ such that \begin{equation} \label{b23} - b_{23} = \rho^{1-\sqrt{q}}.\end{equation}
Substituting this into \eqref{zdegsq}, we obtain
\begin{equation}\label{zdegsqrho}z^{\sqrt{q}} - \rho^{1-\sqrt{q}} z + (x_0^{\sqrt{q}+1} + b_{21} x_0 + b_{22}) = 0,
\end{equation}
which is equivalent to
\begin{equation}\label{zdegsqrhosq}
(\rho z)^{\sqrt{q}} - (\rho z) + \rho^{\sqrt{q}} (x_0^{\sqrt{q}+1} + b_{21} x_0 + b_{22}) = 0.
\end{equation}
Since \eqref{zdegsqrhosq} has no solutions in $\mathbb{F}_q$, Proposition \ref{degsqrtq} implies that $\mathrm{Tr}(\rho^{\sqrt{q}}(x_0^{\sqrt{q}+1} + b_{21} x_0 + b_{22})) \neq 0$ for all $x_0 \in \mathbb{F}_q$. Based on this, we establish the following:\\
\textbf{Claim.} $\rho + \rho^{\sqrt{q}} = 0$.\\
\textit{Proof of Claim.} Suppose, on the contrary, that $\rho + \rho^{\sqrt{q}} \neq 0$. Then it follows that 
\begin{equation}\label{trne0}
(\rho + \rho^{\sqrt{q}})^{\sqrt{q}} \mathrm{Tr}(\rho^{\sqrt{q}}(x_0^{\sqrt{q}+1} + b_{21} x_0 + b_{22})) \neq 0\end{equation}
On the other hand, a direct calculation shows that
\begin{equation}\begin{split}\label{rhoTr}
(\rho &+\rho^{\sqrt{q}})^{\sqrt{q}} \mathrm{Tr}(\rho^{\sqrt{q}}(x_0^{\sqrt{q}+1}+b_{21}x_0+b_{22})) \\
&= (\rho+\rho^{\sqrt{q}})^{\sqrt{q}} \left((\rho+\rho^{\sqrt{q}} )x_0^{\sqrt{q}+1}  + \rho b_{21}^{\sqrt{q}} x_0^{\sqrt{q}} +\rho^{\sqrt{q}} b_{21}x_0 +\rho b_{22}^{\sqrt{q}} +\rho^{\sqrt{q}} b_{22}\right)\\
&= \left((\rho+\rho^{\sqrt{q}} )x_0 + \rho b_{21} \right)^{\sqrt{q}+1} - \left(\rho^{\sqrt{q}+1} b_{21}^{\sqrt{q}+1} - (\rho+\rho^{\sqrt{q}})(\rho b_{22}^{\sqrt{q}}+\rho^{\sqrt{q}} b_{22})\right).
\end{split}\end{equation}
Since the norm map $\mathrm{Nm} : \mathbb{F}_q \to \mathbb{F}_{\sqrt{q}}$ is surjective and$$ \rho^{\sqrt{q}+1} b_{21}^{\sqrt{q}+1}-(\rho+\rho^{\sqrt{q}})(\rho b_{22}^{\sqrt{q}}+\rho^{\sqrt{q}} b_{22}) = \mathrm{Nm}(\rho b_{21}) - \mathrm{Tr}(\rho) \mathrm{Tr}(\rho b_{22}^{\sqrt{q}}) \in \mathbb{F}_{\sqrt{q}}, $$there exists an element $\zeta \in \mathbb{F}_q$ such that $\zeta^{\sqrt{q}+1} = \mathrm{Nm}(\rho b_{21}) - \mathrm{Tr}(\rho) \mathrm{Tr}(\rho b_{22}^{\sqrt{q}})$. Here, $\zeta$ may be zero. If we set $x_0 = \frac{\zeta - \rho b_{21}}{\rho + \rho^{\sqrt{q}}} \in \mathbb{F}_q$, then the expression in \eqref{rhoTr} vanishes, which contradicts \eqref{trne0}.
                                                                          
Thus, we must have $\rho + \rho^{\sqrt{q}} = 0$. This completes the proof of the Claim.

Now, returning to the proof of the Theorem, note that $\rho + \rho^{\sqrt{q}} = 0$ is equivalent to $\rho^{1-\sqrt{q}} = -1$. It then follows from \eqref{b23} that $b_{23} = -\rho^{1-\sqrt{q}} = 1$. Substituting this into \eqref{y=1}, we obtain
\begin{equation}\label{b23=1}
x^{\sqrt{q}+1} + b_{21}x + b_{22} + z + z^{\sqrt{q}} = 0.
\end{equation}
If $b_{21}\ne 0$, substituting $x= - \frac{b_{22}}{b_{21}}$, we have $z+z^{\sqrt{q}} = - (\frac{b_{22}}{b_{21}})^{\sqrt{q}+1}$. Since $- (\frac{b_{22}}{b_{21}})^{\sqrt{q}+1}\in \mathbb{F}_{\sqrt{q}}$ and $\mathrm{Tr}:\mathbb{F}_{{q}}\rightarrow\mathbb{F}_{\sqrt{q}}$ is surjective, there exists an 
 $\mathbb{F}_q$-solution for $z$. This implies the existence of an $\mathbb{F}_q$-rational point on \eqref{b23=1}, which contrads our assumption. Thus we conclude that $b_{21} =0$. So \eqref{b23=1} is reduced to 
\begin{equation}\label{b21=0}
x^{\sqrt{q}+1}  + b_{22} +z  + z^{\sqrt{q}} = 0
\end{equation}
If $b_{22} \in \mathbb{F}_{\sqrt{q}}$, then for any $x_0 \in \mathbb{F}_q$, the value $-(x_0^{\sqrt{q}+1} + b_{22})$ belongs to $\mathbb{F}_{\sqrt{q}}$. By the surjectivity of the trace map, there exists an $\mathbb{F}_q$-solution to \eqref{b21=0}, which contradicts the uniqueness of the point $P$. Thus, $b_{22}$ must be an element of $\mathbb{F}_q \setminus \mathbb{F}_{\sqrt{q}}$. By setting $b_{22} = \eta \in \mathbb{F}_q \setminus \mathbb{F}_{\sqrt{q}}$, we conclude that $A$ is projectively equivalent to
$$ A_2 = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & \eta & 1 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix}. $$
This matrix corresponds to the curve
$$ C_{A_2} : x^{\sqrt{q}+1} + \eta y^{\sqrt{q}+1} + y^{\sqrt{q}} z + y z^{\sqrt{q}} = 0. $$

Now that the equivalence has been established, we conclude by showing the uniqueness of the curve up to projective equivalence. Let
$$ A_3 = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & \eta' & 1 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix} \quad \text{with } \eta' \in \mathbb{F}_q \setminus \mathbb{F}_{\sqrt{q}}, $$
and let $C_{A_3}$ be its corresponding curve:
$$ C_3 :\ x^{\sqrt{q}+1} + \eta' y^{\sqrt{q}+1} + y^{\sqrt{q}} z + y z^{\sqrt{q}} = 0. $$
Since $\mathbb{F}_q$ is a two-dimensional vector space over $\mathbb{F}_{\sqrt{q}}$, the set $\{1, \eta\}$ forms a basis for $\mathbb{F}_q$ over $\mathbb{F}_{\sqrt{q}}$. Hence there exist $\alpha, \beta \in \mathbb{F}_{\sqrt{q}}$ with $\alpha \neq 0$ such that $\eta' = \alpha \eta + \beta$. Recall that the norm and trace maps from $\mathbb{F}_q$ to $\mathbb{F}_{\sqrt{q}}$ are surjective. Therefore, there exist $\zeta, \xi \in \mathbb{F}_q$ such that $\zeta^{\sqrt{q}+1} = \alpha$ and $\xi^{\sqrt{q}} + \xi = \beta$. Note that $\alpha \neq 0$ implies $\zeta \neq 0$. Now, let $T$ be the nonsingular matrix defined by
$$ T = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & \zeta & 0 \\ 0 & \zeta^{-\sqrt{q}} \xi & \zeta^{-\sqrt{q}} \end{pmatrix}. $$
A direct computation yields:
\begin{equation*}\begin{split}
T^* A_2 T &=
\begin{pmatrix} 1&0&0\\0&\zeta^{\sqrt{q}}&\ \zeta^{-1}\xi^{\sqrt{q}} \\0&0&\zeta^{-1} \end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix} 1&0&0\\0&\eta&1\\0&1&0 \end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix} 1&0&0 \\ 0&\zeta&0 \\ 0&\zeta^{-\sqrt{q}}\xi &\ \zeta^{-\sqrt{q}} \end{pmatrix} \\
&=\begin{pmatrix} 1&0&0\\0&\ \zeta^{\sqrt{q}+1}\eta +\xi^{\sqrt{q}}+\xi&1\\0&\ \ 1 &0 \end{pmatrix}
=\begin{pmatrix} 1&0&0\\0&\eta'&1\\0&1&0 \end{pmatrix}= A_3.
\end{split}\end{equation*}
This shows that the matrices $A_2$ and $A_3$ are congruent. Consequently, the curves $C_{A_2}$ and $C_{A_3}$ are projectively equivalent, which completes the proof of uniqueness.

\label{Cab}
Let $C$ be a Hermitian-relative curve. Then $C$ possesses an $\mathbb{F}_q$-rational point that is not an inflexion if and only if $C$ is projectively equivalent to the curve defined by
\begin{equation}\label{eqCab}
C_{a,b} : x^{\sqrt{q}} z + x y^{\sqrt{q}} + b x z^{\sqrt{q}} + a y z^{\sqrt{q}} = 0,
\end{equation}
where $a, b \in \mathbb{F}_q$ and $a \neq 0$. This curve corresponds to the matrix $\begin{pmatrix}0&0&1\\ 1&0&0\\ b&a&0 \end{pmatrix}$.

The points on the curve $C_{a,0}$ lying on the line $z=0$ are $(1,0,0)$ and $(0,1,0)$. For the points in the affine plane ($z \neq 0$), we consider the affine equation:
\begin{equation}\label{Ca0aff}
x^{\sqrt{q}} + x y^{\sqrt{q}} + ay = 0.
\end{equation}
We now determine the number of solutions for $x$ by applying Proposition~\ref{degsqrtq} for each $y \in \mathbb{F}_q$.
If $\mathrm{Nm}(y) \neq 1$, the equation \eqref{Ca0aff} has exactly one solution for $x$. Since there are $q - (\sqrt{q}+1)$ such values of $y$ in $\mathbb{F}_q$ (including $y=0$, where $\mathrm{Nm}(0)=0 \neq 1$), we obtain $(q - \sqrt{q} - 1)$ points in the affine plane. Including the two points at infinity, we have a total of $q - \sqrt{q} + 1$ points so far. Next, we consider the case $\mathrm{Nm}(y) = 1$. There are exactly $\sqrt{q}+1$ such elements in $\mathbb{F}_q$. For these values, the equation \eqref{Ca0aff} has either $\sqrt{q}$ solutions or no solution. By Proposition~\ref{degsqrtq}, it has $\sqrt{q}$ solutions if and only if the constant term $ay$ satisfies $ay = 0$ or $(ay)^{1-\sqrt{q}} = y^{\sqrt{q}}$. Since $\mathrm{Nm}(y)=1$ implies $ay \neq 0$, the condition is equivalent to:\begin{equation}\label{cond_i}(ay)^{1-\sqrt{q}} = y^{\sqrt{q}}.\end{equation}Let $i$ be the number of elements $y \in \mathbb{F}_q$ satisfying $\mathrm{Nm}(y) = 1$ and \eqref{cond_i}. Then the total number of rational points on $C_{a,0}$ is:\begin{equation*}N_q(C_{a,0}) = (q - \sqrt{q} + 1) + i\sqrt{q}.\end{equation*}
The equation $(ay)^{1-\sqrt{q}} = y^{\sqrt{q}}$ is equivalent to $(ay)^{\sqrt{q}-1} = y$ (by raising both sides to the $\sqrt{q}$-th power), or simply, $(ay)^{\sqrt{q}-1} \cdot y^{-1} = 1$. By rearranging the terms, we obtain:
\begin{equation}\label{y_eq}
y^{\sqrt{q}-2} = a^{1-\sqrt{q}}.
\end{equation}
Let $\omega$ be a primitive element of $\mathbb{F}_q$. Since $\mathrm{Nm}(y) = 1$, we can represent $y$ as $y = \omega^{(\sqrt{q}-1)k}$ for some $k \in \{0, 1, \dots, \sqrt{q}\}$. Let $a = \omega^j$. Substituting these into \eqref{y_eq}, we have:
\begin{equation*}
\left( \omega^{(\sqrt{q}-1)k} \right)^{\sqrt{q}-2} = \left( \omega^j \right)^{1-\sqrt{q}},
\end{equation*}
which is equivalent to the following congruence modulo $q-1$:
\begin{equation*}
(\sqrt{q}-2)(\sqrt{q}-1)k \equiv j(1-\sqrt{q}) \pmod{q-1}.
\end{equation*}
Dividing both sides by $\sqrt{q}-1$ (noting that $q-1 = (\sqrt{q}-1)(\sqrt{q}+1)$), we obtain:
\begin{equation*}
(\sqrt{q}-2)k \equiv -j \pmod{\sqrt{q}+1}.
\end{equation*}
Since $\sqrt{q}-2 \equiv -3 \pmod{\sqrt{q}+1}$, the congruence simplifies further to:
\begin{equation}\label{final_cong}
-3k \equiv -j \pmod{\sqrt{q}+1}, \quad \text{or} \quad 3k \equiv j \pmod{\sqrt{q}+1}.
\end{equation}
The number of solutions for $k$ in the congruence $3k \equiv j \pmod{\sqrt{q}+1}$ depends on the value of $ \gcd(\sqrt{q}+1, 3)$.
\begin{enumerate}[{\rm (i)}]
\item If $ \gcd(\sqrt{q}+1, 3) = 1$, the congruence has a unique solution for $k \in \{0, 1, \dots, \sqrt{q}\}$ for any $j$. Thus, $i = 1$, which yields$$ N_q(C_{a,0}) = (q - \sqrt{q} + 1) + 1 \cdot \sqrt{q} = q + 1. $$
\item If $ \gcd(\sqrt{q}+1, 3) = 3$, the congruence has three distinct solutions if $j$ is a multiple of $3$, and no solution otherwise. Since $q-1$ is a multiple of $3$ when $\sqrt{q}+1$ is a multiple of $3$, the condition that $j$ is a multiple of $3$ is equivalent to 
\[ a^{\frac{q-1}{3}} = (\omega^j)^{\frac{q-1}{3}} = (\omega^{q-1})^{\frac{j}{3}} = 1. \]
Consequently, if $a^{\frac{q-1}{3}} = 1$, we have $i = 3$ and 
\[ N_q(C_{a,0}) = (q - \sqrt{q} + 1) + 3\sqrt{q} = q + 2\sqrt{q} + 1. \]
If $a^{\frac{q-1}{3}} \neq 1$, then $i = 0$ and 
\[ N_q(C_{a,0}) = q - \sqrt{q} + 1. \]
\end{enumerate}
This completes the proof of Proposition \ref{N_qCa0}.

\label{prop:Cc}
Any Hermitian-relative curve $C_{a,b}$ with $a, b \in \mathbb{F}_q^*$ defined by
\begin{equation*}
C_{a,b} : x^{\sqrt{q}} z + x y^{\sqrt{q}} + b x z^{\sqrt{q}} + a y z^{\sqrt{q}} = 0
\end{equation*}
is projectively equivalent to a curve $C_c$ for some $c \in \mathbb{F}_q^*$, where $C_c$ is given by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Cc}
C_c : x^{\sqrt{q}}z - cxy^{\sqrt{q}} + cxz^{\sqrt{q}} - yz^{\sqrt{q}} = 0.
\end{equation}
The curve $C_c$ corresponds to the matrix $M_c = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 1 \\ -c & 0 & 0 \\ c & -1 & 0 \end{pmatrix}$. Conversely, any curve $C_c$ with $c \neq 0$ is projectively equivalent to $C_{a,b}$ for some $a, b \in \mathbb{F}_q^*$.

\label{prop:basic}
Let $C_c$ be the curve defined in \eqref{eq:Cc} and $M$ be its associated matrix. The following properties hold:
                            
\begin{enumerate}[{\rm (i)}]
\item $U:=M^{-1}M^* = 
\begin{pmatrix} 0&0&c^{-1}\\ -1&0&1\\ 0&-c^{\sqrt{q}}&c^{\sqrt{q}} \end{pmatrix}
$. 
\item $P(\lambda):=P_U(\lambda) = \lambda^3 - c^{\sqrt{q}} \lambda^2 +  c^{\sqrt{q}} \lambda - c^{\sqrt{q}-1}$ is a characteristic polynomial of the matrix $U=M^{-1}M^*$.
\item The curve $C_c$ meets $z=0$ at $(1,0,0)^t$ and $(0,1,0)^t$, neither of which is an inflexion point.
       
\end{enumerate}

Let $(x_0, y_0, 1)^t$ be an eigenvector of $U$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\lambda_0$. Then we have the following matrix equation:
\begin{equation}\label{matrixeq}
\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & c^{-1} \\ -1 & 0 & 1 \\ 0 & -c^{\sqrt{q}} & c^{\sqrt{q}} \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} x_0 \\ y_0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} = \lambda_0 \begin{pmatrix} x_0 \\ y_0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}.
\end{equation}
This leads to the following system of equations:
\begin{equation}\label{systeq}
\begin{cases}c^{-1} = \lambda_0 x_0 \\ -x_0 + 1 = \lambda_0 y_0 \\ -c^{\sqrt{q}} y_0 + c^{\sqrt{q}} = \lambda_0
\end{cases}\end{equation}
From these relations, we can express $x_0$ and $y_0$ in terms of $\lambda_0$ as follows:
\begin{equation}
x_0 = \frac{1}{c\lambda_0} \quad \text{and} \quad y_0 = 1 - c^{-\sqrt{q}} \lambda_0 = \frac{c\lambda_0 - 1}{c\lambda_0^2}.
\end{equation}

\label{eigenval}
The following properties hold:
\begin{enumerate}[{\rm (i)}]
\item If $(x_0, y_0, 1)^t$ is an inflexion of the curve, then $\frac{1}{cx_0}$ is a root of $P_U(\lambda)$.
\item If the line $y=y_0$ is a $\sqrt{q}$-line of the affine part $C_c^\circ$, then $c^{\sqrt{q}}(1-y_0)$ is a root of $P_U(\lambda)$. Furthermore, this root is a $(\sqrt{q}+1)$-th root of unity.
\end{enumerate}

For $U=M^{-1}M^*$, we have the following:
\begin{enumerate}[{\rm(i)}]\label{U-1*}
\item $U^* $ and $U^{-1}$ are similar to each other. So we have $P_{U^*} (\lambda)= 
                                                        
P_{U^{-1}}(\lambda)$. 
 
\item If $\lambda_0$ is an eigenvalue of $U$, then $\lambda_0^{\sqrt{q}}=\lambda_1^{-1}$ for some eigenvalue $\lambda_1$ of $U$. Note that $\lambda_0$ need not be equal to $\lambda_1$.
\end{enumerate}

\label{musq}
If $\lambda_0 \in \mathbb{F}_q^*$ is a root of $P_{U_c}(\lambda)$ and 
                                  
$\lambda_0 \in\mu_{\sqrt{q}+1}$  , then the corresponding line $y=1-c^{-\sqrt{q}} \lambda_0 \ $ is a $\sqrt{q}$-line of $C_c^\circ$.

\label{prodsum1}
The solutions to the system of equations
$$\begin{cases} \alpha_1\alpha_2 = 1 \\ \alpha_1 + \alpha_2 = 1 \end{cases}$$
over $\mathbb{F}_q$ are given as follows:
\[
\{
\alpha_1 , \alpha_2
\}
=
\begin{cases}
\{ \omega^{\frac{q-1}{3}}, \omega^{\frac{2(q-1)}{3}} \} & p=2 \\
\{ -1, -1 \} & p=3 \\
\{ \omega^{\frac{q-1}{6}}, \omega^{\frac{5(q-1)}{6}} \} & p \neq 2, 3 .
\end{cases}
\]

\label{S1S2S3}
Let three sets as follows;
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
S_1 &= \{(\alpha, \beta, \gamma) \in (\mu_{\sqrt{q}+1})^3 \mid \alpha, \beta, \gamma \text{ are roots of } f_c \text{ for some } c \in \mathbb{F}q^* \}  \\
S_2 &= \{(\alpha, \beta, \gamma) \in (\mu_{\sqrt{q}+1})^3 \mid \alpha+\beta \neq 1, \ \alpha^2 +\alpha\beta+\beta^2 \neq 0, \ \gamma = \frac{\alpha+\beta-\alpha\beta}{\alpha+\beta-1} \} \\
S_3 &= \{(\alpha, \beta, \gamma) \in (\mu_{\sqrt{q}+1})^3 \mid \alpha+\beta = 1, \ \gamma \neq -1 \}.
\end{split}
\end{equation}
Then $S_1$ is the disjoint union of $S_2$ and $S_3$.

\label{conj3roots}
The number of characteristic polynomials that have three distinct simple roots, where only one of them belongs to
                  
$\mu_{\sqrt{q}+1}$,
                                    
is:
\begin{equation*}
\begin{cases}
\dfrac{q-\sqrt{q}-2}{2} \mbox{ when }\sqrt{q} \equiv 0 \pmod{3} \\
\dfrac{q-\sqrt{q}-4}{2} \mbox{ when }\sqrt{q} \equiv 1 \pmod{3} \\
\dfrac{q-\sqrt{q}-2}{2} \mbox{ when }\sqrt{q} \equiv 2 \pmod{3} 
\end{cases}
\end{equation*}

\label{irrpoly}
The number of irreducible characteristic polynomials $f_c$, $c\in\mathbb{F}_q^*$ over $\mathbb{F}_q$  is:
\begin{equation*}
\begin{cases}
\dfrac{q-\sqrt{q}}{3} \mbox{ when }\sqrt{q} \equiv 0 \pmod{3} \\
\dfrac{q-\sqrt{q}}{3} \mbox{ when }\sqrt{q} \equiv 1 \pmod{3} \\
\dfrac{q-\sqrt{q}-2}{3} \mbox{ when }\sqrt{q} \equiv 2 \pmod{3} 
\end{cases}
\end{equation*}
